  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1103 PM CDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nHot and humid conditions will return to the Mid-South on Friday\nas a warm front moves across the region. Behind this boundary,\ndewpoints will quickly increase into the mid and upper 70s with\nafternoon highs reaching low to mid 90s. Areas within and\nsurrounding the Mississippi Delta have a greater than 50 percent\nchance of reaching heat indices of 105 degrees. A Heat Advisory\nwill go into effect at 11 AM for this region.\n\nOutside of the heat, the warm front will also bring increased\nchances for showers and storms as it moves northeast. This\nactivity is not expected to be severe as the better instability\nand shear overlap occurs in the evening hours. By sunset, a\nshortwave trough will swing across the Midwest and eject a cold\nfront towards the Mid-South. Showers and storms will form along\nthe boundary in the evening and persist into the overnight hours.\nAt this time, a low level jet will form and provide around 30 kts\nof bulk shear. In addition, the environment will have plentiful\ninstability and moisture as PWATs hover around the 99th\npercentile. A few storms may become severe, posing a damaging\nwind threat. However, forecast sounding depict increasing\nconvective inhibition around sunset. This may limit the severe\nwindow to a few hours. A Slight to Marginal Risk for severe\nstorms remains in place for tomorrow evening into the overnight\nhours. Damaging winds and heavy rainfall will be the primary\nconcerns.\n\nPleasant conditions will return to the Mid-South on Saturday as\nthe aforementioned front continues south. The coolest day will be\nSunday, where highs are forecast to be in the low to mid 80s. As\nwe move into next week, upper level ridging develops over the\ncentral US. This will increase temperatures locally with highs\nreaching the low to mid 90s by midweek. At this time, additional\nheat headlines may be needed. One aspect to watch next week will\nbe a potential cutoff low spinning over the southeast. This\nfeature may keep low end shower and thunderstorm chances in the\nforecast, but for now the majority of next week appears dry.\n\nANS",
